WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.875
These bars show the array seven, three, five, two. Height means value. Position means index. Bubble, selection and insertion can reach the same sorted result, but their intermediate movements are different.

00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:31.167
Bubble compares neighbouring values. Seven swaps with three, then five, then two. After this complete pass, seven is final on the right. The rest is not sorted yet: five still comes before two.

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:48.958
Selection scans the entire unsorted range without moving its bars. It remembers the smallest value, two, at index three. When the scan finishes, one distant swap exchanges two and seven. Index zero is now final.

00:00:48.958 --> 00:01:08.875
For insertion, this example starts with the sorted prefix three, five, seven. Hold the next key, two, outside the array. Shift seven right, then five, then three. Put two into the gap. These are one way shifts and a final insertion, not swaps.

00:01:08.875 --> 00:01:29.708
For these illustrated operations, bubble makes three comparisons and three swaps. Selection makes three comparisons and one swap. Inserting two into three sorted values makes three comparisons, three shifts and one insertion. State the counting convention before reporting a total.
